# Design & Industrial Engineering Taste Considerations & Philosophical Probes
#
# The marriage of beauty and flow, the dignity of human labor, anti-obsolescence ethics, and wonder probes across systems design.

#>spw_cache_univ_design_ie_taste
#:cache #!design_and_industrial_engineering
#:layer #!pragmatics

^"intent"{
  ~#goal: "Synthesize the aesthetic balance, humanistic ethics, and systems philosophy uniting human-centered industrial design with mathematical industrial engineering."
  ~#resonance: "The sublime beauty of unbroken flow, the dignity of the human worker, resisting planned obsolescence, and compassionate systems architecture"
}

^["the_marriage_of_form_and_flow"]{
  epistemic_unity: "Industrial design asks: 'What brings beauty, delight, and intuitive meaning to the human user?' Industrial engineering asks: 'How can this be realized with mathematical elegance, zero waste, and global reliability?'"
  harmonic_synthesis: #[
    "The Soul and the Skeleton: A product with brilliant industrial design but broken manufacturing is a museum piece nobody can afford; a factory with flawless efficiency producing ugly, frustrating objects wastes human material",
    "Ergonomics as Love: Designing a tool that perfectly cradles the human hand without blisters is not just ergonomics; it is an act of deep physical empathy for another human being",
    "The Aesthetic of the Flowing Line: Standing on the mezzanine of an optimized assembly plant and watching materials glide smoothly without inventory pileups is as aesthetically sublime as watching a symphony orchestra"
  ]
}

^["the_dignity_of_the_human_operator"]{
  rejection_of_taylorism: "Early industrial engineering disgraced itself by treating workers as meat-machines with stopwatches; modern systems engineering exists to liberate workers from danger, fatigue, and meaningless drudgery."
  human_centered_truths: #[
    "Poka-Yoke as Compassion: When a mistake happens on an assembly line, the fault is never the tired worker; the fault is the system designer who failed to make the wrong action physically impossible",
    "Cognitive Sanctuary: An industrial control room or airplane cockpit must be designed so that even under acute terror and panic, the critical information shines with unmistakable clarity",
    "Labor is Not a Variable Cost: Treating employees as disposable spreadsheet rows destroys the informal institutional knowledge that prevents catastrophic industrial failure"
  ]
}

^["the_ethics_of_permanence_against_obsolescence"]{
  moral_reckoning: "The ultimate sin of modern consumer industrial design is planned obsolescence—designing products with glued batteries and artificial software locks meant to die in two years."
  regenerative_principles: #[
    "Design for Repairability: Proudly showing screws instead of toxic adhesives, providing open schematics, and celebrating products that can be repaired with basic hand tools",
    "Material Heirloom Quality: Designing objects whose surfaces age with graceful patina (leather, brass, cast iron, oiled hardwood) rather than scratching into ugly trash",
    "Circular Product Responsibility: If an industrial company profits from manufacturing a million plastic enclosures, it must bear legal and thermodynamic responsibility for taking them back at end-of-life"
  ]
}

^"taste"{
  ~#taste: "To design an object is to write a script for how another human being will physically interact with the world; make that script one of dignity, ease, and beauty."
  ~#wonder: "Why does an assembly line operating with the Toyota 'stop-the-line' principle (Andon cord) produce ten times fewer defects than a factory where managers punish line stoppages?"
}

^"invariants"{
  "Product designs must avoid irreversible adhesive bonding on components subject to routine maintenance or battery degradation.",
  "Industrial engineering workplace designs must comply with NIOSH lifting equations and ergonomic strain limits across all work shifts.",
  "System optimization algorithms must explicitly balance economic profit with employee health metrics and environmental lifecycle emissions.",
}

#>wonder_design_ie_taste_1
?["Can a mass-manufactured smartphone be designed for 100% modular user repair without sacrificing IP68 water resistance or thickness?"]{
  #:depth #!engineering ~#lens(modular_circular_design)
  ~#hypothesis: "Utilizing compressible gasket compression channels, magnetically aligned pogo-pin bus interconnects, and quick-release mechanical latching enables consumers to replace batteries and screens in under 90 seconds while maintaining 1.5-meter water submersion resistance with less than 0.5mm added device thickness."
  !probe{ "Conduct IP68 submersion testing and novice consumer repair timing across modular prototype smartphone enclosures vs glued unibody controls." }
  $%[repairability.screen_replacement_seconds, enclosure.water_ingress_pressure_bar]
}

#>wonder_design_ie_taste_2
?["What psychological factor causes the Andon cord—the ability for any front-line worker to shut down an entire factory—to generate fierce employee pride?"]{
  #:depth #!sociological ~#lens(worker_autonomy_psychology)
  ~#hypothesis: "Granting hourly workers unilateral authority to stop multimillion-dollar production lines restores professional sovereignty and moral agency, reducing voluntary turnover by 60% and transforming workers from alienated laborers into active guardians of quality."
  !probe{ "Conduct longitudinal organizational psychology assessments of worker psychological safety, pride, and defect reporting before and after instituting autonomous Andon line-stop authority." }
  $%[organization.worker_psychological_safety_score, quality.spontaneous_defect_catch_rate]
}
